      text: "There is a particular silence that descends on an exam hall in the minutes before the papers are turned over. Anyone who has sat in it will remember the creak of chairs, the nervous clearing of throats, the invigilator's footsteps measuring out the last of the waiting time. It is a silence that, to all intents and purposes, has not changed in a hundred years, and it is one that educators have long been at pains to defend as the fairest way of measuring what a person knows.\n\nBy no stretch of the imagination is that claim absurd. An exam taken under controlled conditions is a far cry from a coursework essay that may have been polished by an anxious parent or, increasingly, by software. It stands to reason that if everyone answers the same questions in the same time, comparisons between candidates are at least possible. Universities and employers set great store by such comparisons, and on the strength of a single set of results, careers are launched or quietly abandoned.\n\nThe difficulty lies in what exams actually measure. Psychologists who study test anxiety have shown, in no uncertain terms, that a significant minority of candidates perform well below their real ability under timed pressure. Despite decades of research, most systems make few allowances for this. The standard response has been to offer extra time to those with a formal diagnosis, which helps some students but does nothing for the far larger number whose nerves are real but undocumented. They bear the brunt of a system that rewards composure as much as competence.\n\nThere is also a subtler problem. Students who know they will be judged on one morning's performance tend, quite rationally, to study for that morning, to the detriment of deeper understanding. Teachers pay lip service to curiosity while drilling past papers, because that is what their schools will be judged on. Those who take exception to this are told, reasonably enough, that the exam is the only show in town.\n\nWhat, then, is the alternative? One proposal that has made some headway in a number of education systems is disarmingly simple: allow candidates to resit without penalty and record their best result rather than their first. Critics argue that this would devalue qualifications. Advocates point to small studies in which students who knew a second attempt was available reported lower anxiety, and in which first-attempt scores rose rather than fell. The point is not to make exams easier, but to put paid to the idea that a single bad morning should define a young person's future.\n\nIf you are reading this a week before your own exam, none of this will make the silence in the hall any less daunting. Be that as it may, it may help to remember that the people who designed the test were trying, imperfectly, to measure something real, and that you are considerably more than the number they will eventually give you.",
