      minWords: 260, use: ["by no stretch of the imagination", "set great store by", "be that as it may", "stands to reason"],
      model: "It is often claimed that examinations measure not knowledge but the ability to pass examinations. There is some truth in this, but by no stretch of the imagination does it justify abandoning them altogether.\n\nThose who criticise exams make several valid points. Timed tests clearly reward candidates who remain calm under pressure, and some highly capable students underperform simply because of nerves. Moreover, when schools are judged by their results, teachers are tempted to drill past papers instead of encouraging curiosity. What students learn in such conditions is often a set of techniques for answering particular types of question, which they forget soon afterwards.\n\nBe that as it may, exams have advantages that alternatives struggle to match. It stands to reason that when every candidate answers the same questions under the same conditions, results can be compared fairly. Coursework, by contrast, is far harder to verify, particularly now that essays can be produced with the help of software. Universities and employers set great store by qualifications precisely because they trust the conditions under which they were obtained.\n\nFurthermore, the claim that exam technique and knowledge are separate is somewhat misleading. Rarely does a candidate succeed through technique alone. Without a solid grasp of the subject, no amount of strategy will produce a convincing essay or a correct calculation. Technique helps candidates show what they know; it cannot replace knowledge.\n\nIn my view, the solution is not to abolish exams but to reform them. Allowing students to resit without penalty, and designing questions that reward understanding rather than memorisation, would address many of the criticisms. Exams will never be perfect, but they remain, for the moment, the fairest instrument we have."
