      text: "On 28 August 1963, about halfway through his speech at the Lincoln Memorial, Martin Luther King Jr. paused. The text in front of him, drafted and redrafted the night before, contained no mention of a dream. Behind him, the gospel singer Mahalia Jackson is reported to have called out, 'Tell them about the dream, Martin.' King pushed his notes aside and began to extemporise, drawing on passages he had used in sermons and speeches for months. The words that followed are now among the most quoted in the English language. They were, in one sense, entirely unscripted, and in another, the product of years of practice.\n\nThis paradox lies at the heart of public speaking. Audiences prize spontaneity; they want to feel that the speaker is thinking aloud with them rather than reciting. Yet the speakers who seem most spontaneous are very often those who have prepared most thoroughly. Winston Churchill, admired for his ability to command the House of Commons, typically spent hours on a major speech, dictating, revising and setting out the final text in short lines like free verse so that he could see where to pause. A political rival is said to have remarked that Churchill had spent the best years of his life writing impromptu speeches.\n\nWhat, then, does preparation actually involve? Not, for most effective speakers, memorising a script word for word. A memorised text tends to sound memorised: the inflection is too regular, the eye contact too fixed, and if the speaker loses the thread, there is nothing to fall back on but panic. Experienced speakers prepare structure rather than sentences. They know their opening, their three or four main points, the stories that will flesh them out, and the closing lines that will drive the point home. In between, they leave room to respond to the room.\n\nThat responsiveness is what distinguishes a speech from a lecture read aloud. A skilled speaker notices when attention is flagging and changes pace, modulating volume or dropping to an almost conversational tone. A well-timed digression can revive a tired audience, provided the speaker can segue back to the main argument without anyone noticing the join. A question from the floor, handled with aplomb and humour, often wins more goodwill than the most polished closing remarks.\n\nNone of this requires a booming voice or theatrical gestures. Some of the most compelling speakers are terse, almost understated; they move sparingly and let silence do much of the work. What they share is clarity about what they want the audience to leave with, and enough confidence in their material to stop clutching it. The goal, as one speechwriter put it, is not to deliver a text but to have a conversation with a thousand people at once.",
