      title: "Плато продвинутого уровня",
      text: "Every language learner who reaches a high level eventually meets the same unwelcome companion. For years, progress was visible and rewarding: each new tense unlocked new conversations, each month brought films that had been incomprehensible and were now merely difficult. Then, somewhere around the boundary between very good and near-native, the curve flattens. The learner works as hard as ever, perhaps harder, and seems to move not at all. Teachers call it the advanced plateau, and it is responsible for more quiet disenchantment than any irregular verb.\n\nThe plateau is not a misapprehension; it is a mathematical inevitability. The most frequent two or three thousand words of English account for the overwhelming majority of what we hear and read. A learner who knows them can follow most everyday conversation. Every additional word, however, is rarer than the last, and so contributes less to overall comprehension. Learning the thousandth word changes your life; learning the twenty-thousandth changes your understanding of perhaps one sentence a month. Effort and reward, which once seemed commensurate, become wildly mismatched.\n\nGrammar follows a similar pattern. By the time learners reach this stage, the remaining gaps are rarely rules they have never seen. They are rules they know perfectly well but fail to apply under pressure: the article they drop, the preposition borrowed from their first language, the tense that slips when they are tired. These deep-seated habits are hard to dislodge precisely because they rarely impede communication. Native speakers, who are generally polite and often self-effacing about their own grammar, stop correcting them, and without feedback, the errors fossilise.\n\nHow, then, does anyone break through? The research is less unanimous than one might wish, but several strategies appear well-founded. The first is to replace volume with precision. Rather than reading more, advanced learners benefit from reading closely, noticing exactly how a skilled writer phrases something they themselves would have expressed clumsily, and then deliberately borrowing the structure. The second is to seek out uncomfortable feedback: recording oneself, asking a tutor to correct every error rather than only those that obscure meaning, or comparing one's own translation of a passage with a professional one. The third, and perhaps the most important, is to accept that progress at this level is measured in years rather than months.\n\nThere is also some consolation to be found in the nature of the goal. Near-native mastery is not a fixed summit that one either reaches or fails to reach. It is an open-ended process that even native speakers never complete; no one knows every word, and the language itself keeps changing. The learner on the plateau is not stuck at the bottom of a mountain. They are walking along a high ridge with uncharted country on either side, and the view, for those who look up from their vocabulary lists, is remarkable.",
