    title: "Шутка, которая перестала быть смешной",
    text: "In 1729, Jonathan Swift published a short pamphlet proposing that the poor of Ireland might relieve their poverty by selling their children to the rich as food. A Modest Proposal is written in the measured tone of a policy paper, complete with calculations of cost and nourishment, and its horror lies precisely in that composure. No reader with a pulse could have taken it literally. Or so one would assume. Persistent anecdotes suggest that some readers were nonetheless outraged by the author's apparent cruelty, and ever since, the pamphlet has served as a kind of litmus test: to understand it is to understand how irony works; to miss it is to reveal something unsettling about the reader, or perhaps about the times.\n\nSatire has always depended on a shared understanding between writer and audience. The satirist says one thing while meaning another, trusting that readers will register the gap and find in it both humour and judgement. When that trust holds, satire can be devastatingly effective, puncturing the pretensions of the powerful in a way that sober argument rarely manages. When it fails, the results range from the embarrassing to the dangerous. Rarely has that failure been more common than it is today.\n\nConsider what happens to a satirical news story once it leaves the website on which it was published. Stripped of its context, shared as a screenshot and accompanied by an indignant comment, it becomes indistinguishable from the genuine article. Studies of online behaviour have repeatedly found that a striking proportion of readers share headlines without ever opening the story beneath them. Had Swift's pamphlet appeared in such conditions, it is not hard to imagine it being circulated, in all sincerity, as evidence of the depravity of the Anglo-Irish elite, or indeed as a serious proposal by those inclined to agree with it.\n\nThe difficulty is compounded by a phenomenon that commentators have half-jokingly named Poe's Law: the observation that, without a clear signal of intent, it is impossible to create a parody of extreme views that some people will not mistake for the real thing. What makes the law so corrosive is that it cuts both ways. Not only is satire mistaken for sincerity, but sincere extremism, when challenged, retreats into the claim that it was only ever joking. Irony becomes a shield rather than a scalpel.\n\nIt would be tempting to conclude that satire has had its day, that in an age of collapsing context it is simply too risky to say the opposite of what one means. That conclusion, however, rests on a misunderstanding of what satire is for. Its value has never lain in the possibility that everyone will get the joke. It lies in the moment of recognition for those who do: the jolt of seeing an absurdity named. Swift did not write for the readers who missed his point. He wrote for those who, having laughed, found themselves unable to look at the policies of their day in quite the same way.\n\nWhat has changed is not the nature of irony but the responsibility of its audience. If satire depends on readers who notice the gap between what is said and what is meant, then the defence of satire is, in the end, a defence of attentive reading: of checking sources, reading beyond the headline, and tolerating, for a moment, the discomfort of not being sure whether one is being told the truth. That is a demanding standard. It is also, one suspects, the only one compatible with a healthy public conversation.",
