    model: "The two texts take strikingly different views of the future of language learning. The first argues that machine translation and AI writing tools have made learning foreign languages largely unnecessary, since software can now render almost any text instantly and accurately. The second contends that knowing a language is not a skill to be outsourced but a way of thinking, which no machine can acquire on our behalf.\n\nAdmittedly, the first writer has a point. Translation software has improved dramatically, and for many routine purposes, from reading a menu to drafting a business email, it is more than adequate. It would be absurd to insist that a tourist spend years studying Portuguese before a week's holiday in Lisbon. For practical, transactional communication, technology has genuinely lowered the barriers between people.\n\nNotwithstanding these advantages, I find the second position far more convincing. By no stretch of the imagination is communication merely the exchange of information. Not only does a language carry vocabulary and grammar, but it also embodies humour, tact, cultural reference and countless unspoken assumptions. A machine may tell you what a colleague said; it cannot tell you why everyone else in the room laughed, or why your perfectly translated compliment caused offence. Those who rely entirely on software risk understanding every word and missing the point.\n\nMoreover, learning a language changes the learner. Anyone who has struggled to express a thought in another language knows that the process forces us to examine what we actually mean. Hand that struggle to a machine, and we lose one of the most effective forms of mental training available to us. It is also worth remembering that relationships, whether personal or professional, are built on effort. A neighbour who makes the effort to speak your language, however imperfectly, communicates something that no flawless translation can.\n\nIn conclusion, technology will undoubtedly reduce the need for basic language skills in certain situations. However, for anyone who wishes to live, work or form genuine relationships in another culture, learning the language remains not a luxury but a necessity. Were we to abandon it, we would gain convenience and lose understanding."
